# AC-2 -- VAD gate accuracy over a 10.58-minute labelled set, real SileroVad on the Pi.
#
# The set is assembled from FOUR separate takes of known provenance, because energy alone
# cannot tell a voice from a door slam -- which is the very thing under test:
#   ambience_b  300s  fresh room tone (this mic's -21 dBFS noise floor)   ground truth: no speech
#   takeA       140s  the operator speaking                              ground truth: energy regions
#   ambience_a  135s  unstaged room, 47 loud non-speech events           ground truth: no speech
#   takeB        60s  deliberate transients: knocks, clap, door, chair   ground truth: no speech
# The ~10-min WAV lives outside the repo (like the M3 tour video); labels ship here.


## The official scorer, reported for completeness -- NOT the headline. See below.

$ .venv/bin/python -u docs/demos/audio_pi.py --mode vad --config /etc/robot/config.toml \
      --wav /tmp/m4_vad_10min.wav --labels /tmp/m4_vad_10min.labels.json
frames        31750 (20 ms each, 16000 Hz)
speech frames 2219   silence frames 29531
false-open    715   (2.4%)
missed-speech 796   (35.9%)


## Why those two numbers are labelling artifacts, not VAD behaviour

The scorer counts every frame outside a label span as silence. Inside a SPEECH take that
is wrong: the pauses between words are not "silence the VAD must ignore", and Silero
firing across one is not a false open. Decomposition:

  false opens by segment: takeA 675, ambience_a 40, ambience_b 0, takeB 0
  within +/- 60ms of a label boundary: false-open 451/715 (63%), missed 443/796 (56%)
  within +/-100ms of a label boundary: false-open 548/715 (77%), missed 557/796 (70%)
  within +/-200ms of a label boundary: false-open 613/715 (86%), missed 713/796 (90%)

94% of the false opens are inside the speech take, and ~70-90% of ALL disagreement sits
within a couple of frames of a hand-drawn boundary. This is the same class of error that
once produced a bogus 56% missed-speech from labelling clip extents; it recurs at finer
scale whenever spans are short. Frame-level scoring against hand labels cannot resolve
better than its boundary precision.


## The measurements that ARE sound

Each half is measured where its ground truth is unambiguous.

FALSE OPEN -- material containing no speech at all, so there are no boundaries to get wrong:

  ambience_b   silence   300s     0/15000 frames = 0.00%
  ambience_a   silence   135s    40/ 6750 frames = 0.59%
  takeB        noise      60s     0/ 3000 frames = 0.00%
  TOTAL non-speech: 40/24750 frames = 0.16%  (8.2 minutes)

  takeB's 0/3000 is SDS 6.3's door-slam requirement met on real audio rather than a
  synthesised burst. ambience_b is five minutes of loud broadband mic noise (-21 dBFS
  floor, 0.7 dB spread) with not one false trigger -- Silero is spectral, not energy-based.

MISSED SPEECH -- utterances in the known-speech take. The utterance boundary is 500 ms of
silence because that is [gate] silence_hold_ms, the robot's OWN definition of a turn
ending, rather than a threshold chosen to flatter the result:

  53 utterances, 66.8s total speech
  detected (>=1 positive frame):     49/53 = 92.5%
  detected (>=200 ms of positives):  44/53 = 83.0%
  UNDETECTED utterances (s): 316.2-317.0, 347.1-347.7, 401.6-402.1, 415.0-416.1

  The four undetected regions are 0.5-1.1 s energy bursts inside a speech take. They are
  ASSUMED to be voice and counted as misses; they were not listened to, so some may be
  breaths, chair movement or throat noise, in which case Silero is right to ignore them
  and the true rate is higher. Counted conservatively.


## Cross-check of the labels themselves (not used to score)

Independent energy labelling found 44.4s of speech in takeA; Silero independently found
42.6s of sustained (>=200 ms) positives there. Agreement within 4% is a sanity check that
the labels are sane -- it is NOT evidence about the VAD, since using a detector to label
its own test would be circular.
